<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/shakespeares-craft">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Shakespeare (1564–1616) is literature's central figure for a craft reason, not a patriotic
    one: <strong>he expanded what dramatic language could hold</strong> — verse and prose switched
    for meaning, metaphor compressed to puns, characters built from contradictions — and his plays
    have survived four centuries of re-interpretation by being endlessly adaptable.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the verse system, the word-hoard, the contradictions, the afterlife" widget="equationStep" config={shakesCfg} />

  <h2>How to read him well</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Read the speech, not the sentence:</strong> his rhetoric is argument — every major speech is a case being built ("To be or not to be" runs through options like a lawyer's brief).</li>
    <li><strong>Watch the pronouns:</strong> thou/you switches mark intimacy shifts, insults, class moves — Elizabethan English's second person carried social information modern English lost.</li>
    <li><strong>Textual reality:</strong> the plays survive in quartos and the First Folio (1623) — variants, cruxes and collaborative passages (Middleton co-wrote Timon). "Shakespeare" is partly a scholarly reconstruction.</li>
    <li><strong>The authorship "question" is settled:</strong> the evidence for the Stratford man (records, contemporary praise, the Folio) is documentary; alternative candidacies are counterfactual fun, not scholarship.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> The First Folio (1623); Kermode, Shakespeare's Language (2000); the Oxford Shakespeare (with collaboration notes).</p>
</TopicPage>
